elfBilled</code> bits with bitwise OR <p>Value getters are read-only properties: <code>address.city</code>, <code>invoice.xml</code>, <code>totals.taxAmount</code>, <code>response.reportingStatus</code>. A getter is a property when it takes no arguments and returns a string, number, <code>bigint</code>, boolean, enum or optional text. Members that take arguments, return another native object (<code>invoice.data()</code>, <code>invoice.totals()</code>, <code>response.validationResults()</code>) or convert or consume (<code>toPem()</code>, <code>intoXml()</code>, <code>withReason(...)</code>, <code>build()</code>) stay methods, as do <code>len()</code> and <code>isEmpty()</code>. Assigning to a property throws a <code>TypeError</code>.</p> <p>Method names use camelCase. Constructors named <code>new</code> in the underlying API are <code>create</code>, such as <code>Config.create(Environment.NonProduction)</code>; the generated name <code>new_</code> was removed in 0.7 (see migrating to 0.7). Generated constructors (<code>new Config()</code>) are internal. Read the number reference for accepted decimal syntax and rounding rules.</p> <p><code>InvoiceBuilder.build()</code>, <code>InvoiceSigner.sign()</code>, and <code>SignedInvoice.intoXml()</code> consume the object's inner state. Build and signing failures can also consume state; create a new input object before retrying. Subsequent use of consumed state throws an error. Returned invoice data, addresses, totals, and response children are independent snapshots.</p> <p>Objects belong to the WebAssembly instance that created them. Each worker needs its own instance and objects; do not transfer internal pointers between workers. Garbage collection does not guarantee immediate cleanup or erasure of secrets.</p> <p>A library failure throws one <code>FatooraError</code> subclass per error code:</p> Code Class Meaning 1 <code>InvalidInputError</code> A caller-supplied value is malformed or out of range 2 <code>ValidationError</code> Input fails invoice, CSR, or schema rules 3 <code>ParseError</code> Structured input, such as an API response body, could not be parsed 4 <code>XmlError</code> XML could not be serialized, parsed, or processed 5 <code>CryptoError</code> Key, signing, hashing, or certificate processing failed 6 <code>IoError</code> An I/O operation failed 7 <code>NetworkError</code> The HTTP transport failed 8 <code>UnauthorizedError</code> ZATCA rejected the credentials 9 <code>InternalError</code> Internal invariant or consumed-state error 10 <code>ApiError</code> ZATCA returned an error or non-accepted response <p>Each error has <code>code</code>, <code>kind</code> (an <code>ErrorKind</code> value such as <code>ErrorKind.InvalidInput</code>, which is the string <code>'InvalidInput'</code>, or <code>null</code> for a code this version does not know), <code>message</code>, and <code>details</code>, the parsed JSON object with structured information such as validation findings or an HTTP response body. <code>cause</code> is the underlying <code>BindingError</code>. <code>FatooraError.fromCode(code, message, { details })</code> creates the class for a code. Typed errors apply to <code>fatoority/raw</code> too, because it shares the generated classes. See error codes and details.</p> <pre><code>import { Address, FatooraError, InvalidInputError } from 'fatoority';

try {
